diagnosis_text:
A thorough multi-layer investigation found no evidence of any network fault in the Kath1 lab. The helpdesk report describes intermittent, whole-office connectivity drops at gateway 10.1.0.1, which the colleague attributed to the gateway "playing up." The gateway is a VRRP virtual IP managed by keepalived running on two distribution switches, dswa1 (VRRP master, priority 105, holding 10.1.0.1/32 on br0.10) and dswa2 (VRRP backup, priority 1). VRRP statistics confirm that dswa1 has been the stable master since lab startup, having sent 181 advertisements (one every 4 seconds) with zero packet errors, while dswa2 received all 181 and has never once claimed the VIP. No VRRP failover has ever occurred. Physical link health is equally clean: all interfaces on every switch are UP, bond0 between dswa1 and dswa2 reports zero link failures across both LACP slave ports, and carrier-change counters sit at 2 on all physical interfaces — the baseline startup value for Kathara containers. The OSPF fabric is fully converged with 32 routes on all routing devices. No flap scripts, cron jobs, or any active fault-injection process was found anywhere in the topology. TC, pressure, and infra sweeps returned no flags. Applying the hard-gate rule for intermittent claims: with no flap mechanism, no historical traces in dmesg/journal, and a completely healthy current state, the reported symptom is unconfirmed. The colleague's hypothesis that "the gateway is playing up" is not supported by any live evidence.
